  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 257 AM CDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nToday is shaping up to be an unseasonably mild day area-wide. \nHigh temperatures are forecast in the low to mid-70s with breezy\nsouthwesterly winds gusting up to 25 mph this afternoon.\n\nWhile quiet weather is expected locally today, focus remains on\na shortwave impulse tracking eastward into southern Manitoba\nearly this morning. This feature will stay well north of our\nregion today and tonight. However, an associated surface low\nwill push a cold front southward into our area on Thursday.\nAlthough the strongest forcing will pass well to the north,\nunseasonably high low-level moisture (dew points in the mid-to-\nupper 50s) beneath steepening mid-level lapse rates along the \nboundary will bring isolated to widely scattered showers late \ntonight into Thursday.\n\nShower chances (and possibly a few non-severe thunderstorms) \nare forecast to increase to 20-40% across northwestern IL for a \nperiod after midnight tonight. While some of this activity may \nwork its way eastward into parts of the Chicago metro into the \npredawn hours of Thursday, it appears the coverage will be at a \ndiurnal minimum as it moves ahead of the primary frontal \nboundary still to the northwest of the area. However, renewed \nwidely scattered development looks favorable along the front as \nit shifts into the I-80 corridor and points southward Thursday \nafternoon. \n\nAlthough there is not a big push of colder air in the wake of \nthis front, temperatures will be turning cooler (dropping into \nthe 50s by sunset) in its wake Thursday afternoon and evening. \nCooler (more seasonable) weather is then expected to persist \nFriday into the weekend as onshore easterly flow and extensive \ncloud cover (and even rain at times) persists. Highs are \ngenerally forecast to be in the 60s. However, temperatures could\nend up struggling to climb out of the 50s, particularly across \nnorthern IL on Saturday. \n\nEnsemble guidance suggests a series of impulses will track from\nMexico east-northeastward into the Lower Great Lakes region\nduring the weekend, followed by a stronger system moving slowly\nacross the central U.S. next week. This will bring periodic rain\nchances from Friday night through next week. However, dry\nstretches are still expected between systems. Currently, the\nhighest rain chances appear to be Friday night into Saturday,\nand again early to mid-next week. This may allow for a dry\nwindow late Saturday, just in time for outdoor Halloween\nactivities.\n\nKJB",
